WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.800
Most people never think about the machinery inside the software they use.

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.800
And they shouldn't have to.

3
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:11.800
Developers should be able to focus on their creative flow, not wait for their tools.

4
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.200
But that experience is shaped by engineering decisions that are often completely invisible.

5
00:00:15.474 --> 00:00:20.674
Before considering any rewrite or language migration for LUMI,

6
00:00:20.874 --> 00:00:23.674
we measured what actually exists.

7
00:00:23.874 --> 00:00:28.474
We established an empirical baseline across five core local workloads:

8
00:00:28.674 --> 00:00:33.474
stream parsing, workspace discovery, SQLite state, diff patching, and full end-to-end cycles.

9
00:00:33.676 --> 00:00:38.176
The data reveals that every single local operation already executes in milliseconds or less:

10
00:00:38.376 --> 00:00:41.176
from seventeen microseconds for fuzzy diffs,

11
00:00:41.376 --> 00:00:44.476
to under three milliseconds for turn dispatch.

12
00:00:44.758 --> 00:00:46.758
Why does that matter?

13
00:00:46.958 --> 00:00:51.758
Because without a baseline, you cannot know if a proposed rewrite makes things better or simply adds complexity.

14
00:00:51.958 --> 00:00:55.958
Measuring first ensures engineering effort targets constraints that actually impact people,

15
00:00:56.158 --> 00:01:00.158
rather than optimizing what is already fast.

16
00:01:00.381 --> 00:01:04.981
The goal of performance engineering isn't to make a synthetic benchmark look impressive.

17
00:01:05.181 --> 00:01:08.581
It's to build software that serves people better.

18
00:01:08.781 --> 00:01:11.581
Good infrastructure doesn't need to be visible to be valuable.

19
00:01:11.781 --> 00:01:13.381
But its benefits should always be measurable.
